From Signals to Product-Shaped Opportunities
Eriks Briedis
@eriks_b
·
Jul 24

What changed when I stopped treating every market signal as the beginning of a product.
Give an LLM enough reviews, complaints, job posts, interview notes, forum threads, or competitor pages, and it will produce a polished backlog of product ideas.
I built an internal, self-hosted research system around that capability. The first version used a straightforward pipeline:
Collect evidence → extract workflow pain → normalise it → cluster similar signals → generate ideas
It produced plenty of plausible ideas. The problem was that the polish often made the supporting evidence look stronger than it really was.
A one-off complaint could become a convincing product brief. An observation with no clear buyer could turn into a software concept. Several weak signals could be combined into something that looked well supported.
The system was answering:
What workflow pains were mentioned?
What I actually needed to know was:
Which workflow problems are specific, recurring, consequential, tied to an identifiable buyer, and worth validating?
Those are different questions.
Answering the second required another layer between market evidence and product generation.
From signal to opportunity to product hypothesis
The original pipeline treated extracted pain points as the main unit of research.
A structured signal might capture the affected person, workflow, workaround, urgency, tools, and original source. That was useful. It turned messy material into evidence that could be searched, compared, and clustered.
But a structured signal still showed only that something had been mentioned.
Consider this example:
Administrative staff at small construction firms manually classify and file subcontractor compliance documents received by email.
This tells us that someone is doing manual work.
It does not tell us how often the task occurs, whether it causes a meaningful problem, who owns the process, what mistakes cost, whether the issue appears across multiple firms, or whether anyone would pay to change it.
The signal may be useful, but it is not yet a product-shaped opportunity.
That distinction is easy to lose because LLMs are good at completing plausible narratives. Once a model sees a manual workflow, it can infer a buyer, a consequence, a product, and a business case.
The result can sound coherent even when much of that coherence came from the model rather than the evidence.
The revised pipeline separates three concepts:
A signal is a unit of market evidence.
A problem opportunity is a structured hypothesis that a recurring workflow failure creates a meaningful consequence for a particular buyer.
A product hypothesis is a proposed intervention in that workflow.
Using the same example, the signal might become this problem opportunity:
Small construction firms repeatedly spend administrative time classifying and filing emailed subcontractor documents, while inconsistent naming and storage practices create retrieval delays and compliance risk.
This is more useful because it identifies the market, workflow, recurring task, affected artifact, and possible consequence.
But some of it may still be inferred. “Repeatedly,” “retrieval delays,” and “compliance risk” are not established unless the source evidence supports them.
The opportunity needs to preserve that distinction instead of presenting the entire statement as fact.
A product hypothesis might then be:
An email intake assistant that detects subcontractor insurance certificates, proposes the correct project folder and filename based on the firm’s existing conventions, and requires confirmation before filing.
This is no longer a broad idea about construction document management. It is a specific intervention at a particular point in the workflow.
That makes it possible to evaluate directly:
Can the document be classified reliably?
Is the required project context available?
Is there enough volume for the problem to matter?
Would users trust a proposal-and-confirmation workflow?
Its value can also be tested against concrete outcomes such as time saved, faster retrieval, and fewer filing errors.
The further the pipeline moves from observation towards a proposed product, the more evidence it should require.
Interpretation became explicit
The revised pipeline no longer clusters raw pain statements and immediately asks for product ideas.
It first tries to form a coherent opportunity by recording who owns the workflow, where it breaks, what consequence follows, and which claims were observed rather than inferred.
This does not prove that the opportunity is real. It makes the system’s interpretation visible.
Previously, missing context could disappear during summarisation. A cluster might contain several loosely related complaints, but its final description could still present a clear buyer and recurring problem even when neither had been established.
The opportunity record makes those assumptions explicit rather than burying them in a generated summary.
It also gives the pipeline a more useful object to compare and cluster. Instead of grouping similar phrases, it can compare buyers, workflows, failures, consequences, and unresolved questions.
Product generation became constrained
The research flow became:
Raw evidence → structured signals → problem opportunities → opportunity clusters → product hypotheses → validation candidates
Product ideas still exist in the system. They are simply generated later.
The earlier stages try to establish whether there is a coherent buyer, workflow, failure, and consequence before asking what should be built.
Instead of asking:
What products could solve these complaints?
the system can ask:
Given this buyer, workflow, recurring failure, consequence, and available data, what is the narrowest plausible intervention?
The second question gives the model useful constraints. It also produces hypotheses that are easier to reject.
“AI-powered document management for construction” is difficult to evaluate because it has no clear workflow boundary or measurable outcome.
A hypothesis about identifying one document type, proposing a destination, and requiring confirmation can fail for specific reasons. Classification may be unreliable. The necessary context may not be available. Filing conventions may vary too much. The workflow volume may be too low.
That is useful.
The goal is not to make every idea sound better. It is to make weak ideas easier to identify.
The evidence remained attached
A generated idea does not automatically become a product project.
When a candidate moves into validation, it keeps the chain that produced it:
product candidate ← product hypothesis ← opportunity ← source signals
The candidate remains connected to its source evidence, assumptions, proposed scope, unresolved risks, and later validation findings.
The generated product description cannot quietly replace the incomplete evidence behind it.
Moving a candidate forward does not mean the opportunity has been proven. It means only that the hypothesis is coherent and supported well enough to justify direct validation.
The pipeline still does not prove a market
Adding an opportunity layer improves the quality of the research output. It does not show that buyers consider the problem important or that it occurs often enough to matter.
It also does not show that the proposed intervention fits the real workflow or that anyone will pay for it.
Those questions require direct validation.
Public complaints, job posts, reviews, and forum discussions can reveal useful patterns. They are not substitutes for observing workflows or speaking to buyers.
The pipeline should produce a better starting point for those conversations: a clearer buyer, a narrower problem, a specific intervention, visible supporting evidence, explicit assumptions, and a next test that could disprove the hypothesis.
Fewer ideas can be a better result
Idea volume was never the constraint. The difficult part was deciding which possibilities deserved validation without allowing plausible language to stand in for evidence.
Adding the opportunity layer preserved the source evidence, exposed the assumptions, and forced each proposed intervention into a specific workflow.
Defining the opportunity as a separate research object was the first change. The next challenge was deciding what context it needed and how much evidence it should require.
The result is not customer proof.
It is a smaller set of product hypotheses that are specific enough to test and bounded enough to reject when the evidence fails.